.TH DOTFILES 1 "March 2026" "version 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dotfiles \- manage dotfiles with symlinks and git
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dotfiles
.br
.B dotfiles status
.br
.B dotfiles add
.I path
[\fIpath\fR ...]
.br
.B dotfiles update
[\fB\-m\fR \fImessage\fR]
.br
.B dotfiles clone
.I repository
[\fIpath\fR]
.br
.B dotfiles link
[\fIname\fR ...]
.br
.B dotfiles clean
[\fIname\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dotfiles
is a CLI tool for managing dotfiles in a git repository using symlinks.
It tracks which dotfiles in the current directory are managed (symlinked into
the dotfiles repo) and which are not. Files are moved into the repo and
replaced with relative symlinks, matching the convention used by GNU Stow.
.PP
The dotfiles repository location is determined by the
.B DOTFILES_REPO_PATH
environment variable, falling back to
.IR ~/.dotfiles .
.PP
The repo structure mirrors home-relative paths: a file at
.I repo/.zshrc
maps to
.IR ~/.zshrc ,
and
.I repo/work/project/.claude
maps to
.IR ~/work/project/.claude .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (none)
List all dotfiles in the current directory, showing whether each is managed
(symlinked into the repo) or unmanaged. Displays the symlink target for
managed files.
.TP
.B status
Show a git-status-style view of managed dotfiles. Displays pending changes
for managed files, the count of unpushed commits, and lists unmanaged
dotfile candidates.
.TP
.B add \fIpath\fR [\fIpath\fR ...]
Move one or more files into the repo and replace them with relative
symlinks. Accepts relative paths, absolute paths, or ~/paths. Each file
must be under
.IR $HOME .
The file is stored at the corresponding home-relative path within the repo.
If symlink creation fails after the move, the file is rolled back to its
original location.
.TP
.B update \fR[\fB\-m\fR \fImessage\fR]
Stage, commit, and push changes to managed dotfiles. When run outside the
repo directory, only files managed from the current directory are included.
When run inside the repo, all changes are included. If
.B \-m
is not provided, an auto-generated commit message listing the changed files
is used.
.TP
.B clone \fIrepository\fR [\fIpath\fR]
Clone a remote dotfiles repository and restore all symlinks by walking
the repo contents. The default destination is
.IR ~/.dotfiles .
This is the primary way to bootstrap dotfiles on a new machine.
.TP
.B link \fR[\fIname\fR ...]
Restore symlinks by walking the repo contents. Creates relative symlinks
for all files in the repo, or only the specified names. Skips entries that
are already correctly linked. Useful after cloning or if a symlink was
accidentally deleted.
.TP
.B clean \fR[\fIname\fR ...]
Remove symlinks for managed dotfiles in the current directory and copy the
files back from the repo. If names are specified, only those are cleaned;
otherwise all managed dotfiles in the current directory are cleaned.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-m " \fImessage\fR"
Commit message for the
.B update
command. If omitted, a message is auto-generated from the list of changed
files.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DOTFILES_REPO_PATH
Path to the dotfiles git repository. Defaults to
.IR ~/.dotfiles .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dotfiles
List dotfiles in the current directory with their managed status.
.TP
.B dotfiles status
Show pending changes and unmanaged candidates.
.TP
.B dotfiles add .zshrc .vimrc
Move .zshrc and .vimrc into the repo and create symlinks.
.TP
.B dotfiles update
Auto-commit and push all pending dotfile changes.
.TP
.B dotfiles update \-m \(dqadd zsh config\(dq
Commit and push with a custom message.
.TP
.B dotfiles clone git@github.com:user/dotfiles.git
Clone dotfiles repo to ~/.dotfiles and restore all symlinks.
.TP
.B dotfiles clone git@github.com:user/dotfiles.git ~/my-dotfiles
Clone to a custom path.
.TP
.B dotfiles link
Restore all symlinks from repo contents.
.TP
.B dotfiles link .zshrc
Restore only the .zshrc symlink.
.TP
.B dotfiles clean .bashrc
Remove the .bashrc symlink and restore the original file.
.TP
.B dotfiles clean
Remove all managed symlinks in the current directory.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred.
